 ## Overview
-The NLR Baseline serves as a reference implementation for Neural Lumigraph Representations, optimizing a neural network to predict density and color for novel view synthesis. It differs from its siblings by utilizing a specific neural architecture designed for lumigraph-like representations, distinct from the MLP-based NeRF [13271-nerf-training-setup.md] or the generative StyleGAN2 [12972-stylegan2-training-configuration.md] approaches. It operates within the standard differentiable volume rendering loop [52-volume-rendering.md], minimizing photometric loss between rendered and ground truth images.
+The NLR Baseline serves as a reference implementation for Neural Lumigraph Representations, a method for novel view synthesis that learns a continuous radiance field parameterized by neural networks. It solves the problem of synthesizing high-quality novel views from a sparse set of input images by optimizing a neural network to predict density and color along rays. It is distinguished from its siblings by its specific architecture for handling lumigraph-like representations and its baseline status against which meta-learning variants (like MetaNLR) are compared.
 
 ## How it works
-The NLR Baseline follows the shared principles of the parent family [13740-training-setup-and-baselines.md], relying on stochastic gradient descent to minimize Mean Squared Error (MSE) between rendered pixels and ground truth.
+The method operates within the differentiable volume rendering pipeline [52-volume-rendering.md]. It minimizes a photometric loss (Mean Squared Error) between rendered pixel colors and ground truth images via stochastic gradient descent.
 
